1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:03,430
January fifteenth, nineteen nineteen.

2
00:00:03,680 --> 00:00:08,060
Boston, Massachusetts. An unusually warm afternoon in the North

3
00:00:08,080 --> 00:00:10,990
End. Children are walking home from school.

4
00:00:11,040 --> 00:00:12,910
Workers are on their lunch break.

5
00:00:12,960 --> 00:00:15,340
And above them, on a hill by the harbor,

6
00:00:15,360 --> 00:00:18,540
stands a steel tank holding two point three million

7
00:00:18,560 --> 00:00:20,310
gallons of molasses.

8
00:00:20,400 --> 00:00:22,630
It has been groaning for years.

9
00:00:22,720 --> 00:00:24,950
Today, it will not hold.

10
00:00:25,654 --> 00:00:28,394
The tank belonged to the Purity Distilling Company,

11
00:00:28,414 --> 00:00:31,204
and it stood at five twenty-nine Commercial Street.

12
00:00:31,334 --> 00:00:32,754
Fifty feet tall.

13
00:00:32,774 --> 00:00:34,164
Ninety feet wide.

14
00:00:34,214 --> 00:00:37,154
Built in nineteen fifteen to store molasses brought in

15
00:00:37,174 --> 00:00:38,954
by ship. Why?

16
00:00:38,974 --> 00:00:41,954
Because molasses can be turned into industrial alcohol,

17
00:00:41,974 --> 00:00:45,404
and alcohol was also a key ingredient in munitions.

18
00:00:46,120 --> 00:00:48,150
There was a reason for the hurry.

19
00:00:48,280 --> 00:00:51,940
The next day, January sixteenth, the Eighteenth Amendment would

20
00:00:51,960 --> 00:00:54,830
be ratified. Prohibition was coming.

21
00:00:55,080 --> 00:00:57,380
The company wanted to make as much alcohol as

22
00:00:57,400 --> 00:00:59,980
it could. The tank was built fast,

23
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:01,390
and it was barely tested.

24
00:01:01,640 --> 00:01:04,260
The treasurer who oversaw it, Arthur Jell,

25
00:01:04,280 --> 00:01:05,870
had no engineering training.

26
00:01:06,120 --> 00:01:08,140
He never even filled it with water to check

27
00:01:08,160 --> 00:01:10,740
for leaks. When the tank was finally filled with

28
00:01:10,760 --> 00:01:13,980
molasses, it leaked so badly that the company simply

29
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:16,350
painted it brown, to hide the stains.

30
00:01:16,440 --> 00:01:19,580
Neighbors came with buckets to collect the sweet drips.

31
00:01:19,600 --> 00:01:22,750
Every time it was filled, the walls groaned.

32
00:01:22,800 --> 00:01:27,284
Nobody listened. On January fourteenth, a ship delivered a

33
00:01:27,304 --> 00:01:29,574
fresh load of warm molasses.

34
00:01:29,704 --> 00:01:31,174
And the weather changed.

35
00:01:31,224 --> 00:01:33,924
In just two days, the temperature in Boston had

36
00:01:33,944 --> 00:01:37,164
jumped from about two degrees to over forty.

37
00:01:37,184 --> 00:01:39,364
At about twelve thirty on the fifteenth,

38
00:01:39,384 --> 00:01:40,734
the tank gave way.

39
00:01:40,824 --> 00:01:43,014
Witnesses felt the ground shake.

40
00:01:43,064 --> 00:01:45,854
They heard a roar, like an elevated train.

41
00:01:45,944 --> 00:01:48,174
Some said it sounded like a machine gun.

42
00:01:48,264 --> 00:01:50,044
That was the sound of the rivets,

43
00:01:50,064 --> 00:01:51,534
shooting out of the steel.

44
00:01:52,233 --> 00:01:56,933
Two point three million gallons, about thirteen thousand tons,

45
00:01:56,953 --> 00:01:58,783
poured out all at once.

46
00:01:58,873 --> 00:02:02,093
A wave of molasses, up to twenty-five feet high

47
00:02:02,113 --> 00:02:04,933
at its peak, raced down Commercial Street at around

48
00:02:04,953 --> 00:02:06,743
thirty-five miles per hour.

49
00:02:06,793 --> 00:02:09,463
It was forty percent heavier than water.

50
00:02:09,513 --> 00:02:11,813
It tipped a streetcar off its tracks.

51
00:02:11,833 --> 00:02:14,373
It slammed steel panels from the tank into the

52
00:02:14,393 --> 00:02:16,853
elevated railway, and bent the girders.

53
00:02:16,873 --> 00:02:18,933
It lifted a truck and threw it into the

54
00:02:18,953 --> 00:02:22,133
harbor. Buildings were torn from their foundations,

55
00:02:22,153 --> 00:02:26,764
and crushed. Then, the molasses did something water never

56
00:02:26,784 --> 00:02:30,374
does. It cooled, and it thickened.

57
00:02:30,504 --> 00:02:33,284
Scientists call it a non-Newtonian fluid.

58
00:02:33,304 --> 00:02:35,564
Under pressure, it flows fast.

59
00:02:35,584 --> 00:02:37,684
Once it settles, it turns thick,

60
00:02:37,704 --> 00:02:39,324
and it holds on.

61
00:02:39,344 --> 00:02:42,444
The Boston Post wrote that horses died like flies

62
00:02:42,464 --> 00:02:45,244
on flypaper. The more they struggled,

63
00:02:45,264 --> 00:02:46,924
the deeper they sank.

64
00:02:46,944 --> 00:02:48,804
People were caught the same way.

65
00:02:48,824 --> 00:02:51,404
A boy named Anthony di Stasio was carried on

66
00:02:51,424 --> 00:02:53,844
the crest of the wave, then rolled like a

67
00:02:53,864 --> 00:02:56,724
pebble. He heard his mother call his name.

68
00:02:56,744 --> 00:02:59,404
He could not answer, because his throat was packed

69
00:02:59,424 --> 00:03:03,345
with molasses. The first to arrive were one hundred

70
00:03:03,365 --> 00:03:06,145
and sixteen cadets from the training ship Nantucket,

71
00:03:06,165 --> 00:03:09,065
docked nearby. They ran several blocks,

72
00:03:09,085 --> 00:03:11,465
and waded into the knee-deep syrup to pull out

73
00:03:11,485 --> 00:03:17,065
survivors. Police, soldiers, sailors and Red Cross nurses followed.

74
00:03:17,085 --> 00:03:19,945
Some nurses dove right into the molasses.

75
00:03:19,965 --> 00:03:23,105
Doctors set up a hospital in a nearby building.

76
00:03:23,125 --> 00:03:25,865
Rescuers searched for four days.

77
00:03:25,885 --> 00:03:28,185
Some victims were so coated that they could not

78
00:03:28,205 --> 00:03:31,412
be recognized. Others were swept into the harbor,

79
00:03:31,432 --> 00:03:34,115
and were found three or four months later.

80
00:03:34,826 --> 00:03:37,136
Twenty-one people died that day.

81
00:03:37,306 --> 00:03:38,486
Not from water.

82
00:03:38,506 --> 00:03:39,696
Not from fire.

83
00:03:39,786 --> 00:03:43,126
From molasses. About one hundred and fifty more were

84
00:03:43,146 --> 00:03:46,846
injured. Cleanup crews used salt water from fireboats to

85
00:03:46,866 --> 00:03:49,206
wash the streets, and sand to soak up the

86
00:03:49,226 --> 00:03:52,726
rest. The harbor stayed brown until summer.

87
00:03:52,746 --> 00:03:55,966
Molasses was carried by shoes onto trains,

88
00:03:55,986 --> 00:03:58,406
into homes, onto telephones.

89
00:03:58,426 --> 00:04:01,376
It was said that everything a Bostonian touched was

90
00:04:01,466 --> 00:04:04,555
sticky. Then came the question:

91
00:04:04,575 --> 00:04:06,005
who was to blame?

92
00:04:06,175 --> 00:04:09,995
The company, now owned by United States Industrial Alcohol,

93
00:04:10,015 --> 00:04:12,565
said anarchists had blown up the tank.

94
00:04:12,735 --> 00:04:15,125
One hundred and nineteen residents sued.

95
00:04:15,375 --> 00:04:17,835
It was one of the first class-action lawsuits in

96
00:04:17,855 --> 00:04:21,035
Massachusetts. After three years of hearings,

97
00:04:21,055 --> 00:04:24,395
the court-appointed auditor, Hugh Ogden, ruled that the company

98
00:04:24,415 --> 00:04:27,915
was responsible. It paid six hundred and twenty-eight thousand

99
00:04:27,935 --> 00:04:31,435
dollars. The families of the dead reportedly received about

100
00:04:31,455 --> 00:04:33,285
seven thousand dollars each.

101
00:04:33,991 --> 00:04:35,901
Later studies found the truth.

102
00:04:36,231 --> 00:04:39,091
In twenty fourteen, engineers found that the steel was

103
00:04:39,111 --> 00:04:41,331
only half as thick as it should have been,

104
00:04:41,351 --> 00:04:42,621
and that it was brittle.

105
00:04:42,871 --> 00:04:44,781
Cracks had started at the rivets.

106
00:04:45,191 --> 00:04:48,451
In twenty sixteen, researchers at Harvard tested a scale

107
00:04:48,471 --> 00:04:50,301
model with cold corn syrup.

108
00:04:50,551 --> 00:04:54,691
Their conclusion: yes, the wave really was that fast.

109
00:04:54,711 --> 00:04:57,531
And yes, it really did thicken quickly enough to

110
00:04:57,551 --> 00:04:59,661
trap people before they could be freed.

111
00:05:00,360 --> 00:05:02,590
The company never rebuilt the tank.

112
00:05:03,000 --> 00:05:05,980
Today, a baseball field and a playground stand on

113
00:05:06,000 --> 00:05:08,340
the spot, and a small plaque at the park

114
00:05:08,360 --> 00:05:09,910
remembers what happened.

115
00:05:10,040 --> 00:05:13,220
For decades, people said that on warm summer days,

116
00:05:13,240 --> 00:05:16,230
the North End still smelled of molasses.

117
00:05:16,520 --> 00:05:18,510
The disaster changed the law.

118
00:05:18,600 --> 00:05:21,150
After it, buildings needed to be approved by a

119
00:05:21,240 --> 00:05:23,230
licensed architect and engineer.

120
00:05:23,400 --> 00:05:26,180
On the hundredth anniversary, people stood in a circle

121
00:05:26,200 --> 00:05:28,660
on the field, marking the edge of the tank,

122
00:05:28,680 --> 00:05:31,950
and read aloud the names of all twenty-one victims.

123
00:05:32,659 --> 00:05:35,249
A tank. A warm day.

124
00:05:35,379 --> 00:05:38,009
A warning that nobody wanted to hear.

125
00:05:38,179 --> 00:05:40,319
This is the story they don’t teach you in

126
00:05:40,339 --> 00:05:41,129
school.

